1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,110
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:06,320
Today, we're exploring the sunk cost fallacy

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:10,190
a cognitive bias that often leads to irrational decision-making.

4
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:12,760
The sunk cost fallacy occurs

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:18,398
when individuals continue an endeavor or consume a product as a result of previously invested resources,

6
00:00:18,398 --> 00:00:21,260
such as money, time, or effort

7
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,260
even when the current costs outweigh the future benefits.

8
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:30,040
Essentially, people feel that they shouldn't "waste" what they've already spent.

9
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:34,209
For instance, imagine you bought a ticket to a three-hour movie.

10
00:00:34,209 --> 00:00:39,515
After thirty minutes, you realize the movie is terrible and you aren't enjoying it.

11
00:00:39,515 --> 00:00:43,360
However, most people will stay until the end

12
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:45,200
because they already paid for the ticket.

13
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,333
In reality, the money is gone. It's a "sunk cost"-and by staying,

14
00:00:50,333 --> 00:00:54,000
they are only wasting two more hours of their time.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,080
In business, this fallacy can be even more damaging.

16
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:01,584
Companies might continue to fund a failing project just

17
00:01:01,584 --> 00:01:05,478
because they have already invested millions of dollars into it,

18
00:01:05,478 --> 00:01:06,620
rather than cutting their losses

19
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:10,150
and redirecting resources to more profitable ventures.

20
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:14,200
Understanding this bias is crucial for making objective choices.

21
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:17,540
Next, we will discuss how to recognize

22
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:22,030
and avoid the sunk cost fallacy in long-term financial planning.
